---
<Layout title="About the Hub concept" active="about">
  <section class="page-intro"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Concept demonstrator</p><h1>About this evidence hub demo<span class="title-dot">.</span></h1><p>This prototype uses fictional community health records to demonstrate a searchable library, a coverage map and links to example source sheets.</p></div></section>
  <div class="container about-grid"><nav class="contents-nav" aria-label="On this page"><p class="eyebrow">On this page</p><a href="#approach">Our approach</a><a href="#assessments">Reading assessments</a><a href="#updating">Keeping it useful</a><a href="#demo">About this demo</a><a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a></nav><div class="prose">
    <section id="approach"><h2>Reading different kinds of evidence</h2><p>A research study, a community account and a practitioner’s reflection answer different questions. The demo presents these alongside evaluations, with methods and limitations for each record.</p><p>The library helps people find a relevant record. The map shows where this collection has coverage and where it has gaps. Each record carries a summary, an assessment with a reason, limitations and a route back to its source.</p><div class="pull-quote">Read each example assessment with its reason.</div><p>A count is not a finding, a label is not a verdict, and an empty cell is not proof that an intervention has no effect.</p></section>
    <section id="assessments"><h2>Read the explanation, not just the label.</h2><p>The demo uses three illustrative assessment labels: <strong>More consistent</strong>, <strong>Mixed</strong> and <strong>Limited</strong>. Every label has a written reason and relates to the particular claim and context in its record.</p><p>These are supplied example judgements, not automated scores or an agreed appraisal framework. They are not inferred from a record’s knowledge type. In the proposed service, reviewers would set the assessments against criteria agreed with the commissioning organisation and community partners.</p></section>
    <section id="updating"><h2>Publishing from the register</h2><p>One structured register generates the library, evidence map and individual pages. Visitors see changes only after the site has been rebuilt and deployed.</p><ol class="workflow"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Prepare a record</h3><p>Add a summary, classifications, source details and a reasoned assessment to the register.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Check and approve</h3><p>Validate required fields and agreed vocabulary. Only records with release approval enter the public build.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Publish the update</h3><p>Rebuild the static site and deploy the new build. This publishes the updated library, map and record pages together.</p></div></li></ol><div class="notice"><strong>What works in this demo:</strong> edit the local JSON register, rebuild the site and deploy the new build to publish the changes. The proposed SharePoint-to-GitHub publishing connection is a future integration; this demonstration does not connect to any organisation’s systems.</div><a class="text-link" href="/data/evidence.json" download>Download the public demo register <Icon name="download"/></a></section>
    <section id="demo"><h2>A demonstration, not an evidence base.</h2><p>This prototype was prepared to illustrate the University of Edinburgh’s proposed approach to an Community Evidence Hub. It is not an official website or an approved design.</p><p><strong>All records, findings, samples, assessments, programme names, producers and funders are fictional.</strong> The theme structure is illustrative. Northvale and Eastmere are invented places used only to demonstrate filtering; no local claims should be inferred.</p><p>The fictional fixture text was prepared with AI assistance for interface testing. It is not evidence synthesis or researcher-authored project content. The proposed live Hub would use human-written and reviewed evidence content; AI coding tools would support software development.</p><p>There are no analytics, external fonts or third-party scripts. The demonstration can be served as static files on an Azure VM.</p></section>
    <section id="accessibility"><h2>Keyboard access and map alternatives</h2><p>The prototype uses semantic headings, labelled controls, visible keyboard focus, text alongside colour and a native data table for the evidence map. A list view provides another way to explore the same records.</p><p>This is an early prototype and has not had an independent accessibility audit or disabled-user testing. The proposed project includes specialist audit, remediation and retesting.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="/evidence/">Explore the demonstration <Icon/></a></section>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
